          "text": "Now I have confirmed the API facts. Let me compose the plan and ask clarifying questions. The user said duplication was unintentional, ignore.\n\nLet me now think carefully about what the plan should contain, and what questions to ask.\n\nKey facts confirmed:\n- from_chars integer: pattern identical to strtol, EXCEPT: \"0x\"/\"0X\" not recognized if base 16; only minus sign recognized (not plus); leading whitespace not ignored.\n- from_chars: if parsed value not representable → ec=result_out_of_range, ptr at first non-matching char, value unmodified.\n- strtol: discards whitespace; accepts plus AND minus; with base 10 accepts only decimal digits; if out of range → ERANGE + returns LONG_MAX/LONG_MIN; if no conversion → 0, endptr=str.\n\nSo for t1-t2:\n- Difference 1: plus sign: strtol accepts leading '+', from_chars doesn't. Already handled by manual '+' skip on C++ side. Aligned.\n- Difference 2: leading whitespace: strtol skips, from_chars doesn't. Already handled by trim on both sides. Aligned.\n- Difference 3: hex \"0x\": with base 10 explicitly, strtol does NOT recognize 0x (only base 16 or 0). from_chars base 10 also doesn't. **Aligned already.** (Important: don't copy is_hex_prefix to t1-t2!)\n- Difference 4: **overflow**: strtol → ERANGE + clamped LONG_MAX/MIN (parse \"succeeds\", endptr at end); from_chars → result_out_of_range. This is the divergence. C currently ignores errno → range check → USAGE(64); C++ → DATA(65).\n- Difference 5: type width: `long` (C) vs `int` (C++). On MSVC both 32-bit; on Linux long 64-bit. Affects the boundary platform-dependently.\n\nSo the plan:\n\n## Analysis / divergence\nOnly real divergence = integer overflow. Plus a latent cross-platform type-width issue.\n\n## Architectural axes (to name before edits)\n1. Error model: how to classify \"valid integer but not representable\" — as DATA (not number) vs USAGE (domain range)? Consistency with t3-t4 says out_of_range → DATA 65 and a dedicated message. Also for 