eader, or reuse only NOT_NUMBER/OUT_OF_RANGE numbers.\n\nHmm. Actually maybe simplest: the parser returns an enum { INT_PARSE_OK, INT_PARSE_NOT_NUMBER, INT_PARSE_OUT_OF_RANGE }. For C++ use expected<int32_t, IntParseError>.\n\nBut the user's established style in geometry uses plain int constants. Could define in a new common header `common/parse_codes.h`? This is getting heavy.\n\nAlternative: put the integer parser in the existing `geometry` modules? No — semantics wrong (geometry is points).\n\nGiven scope, I think a new pair `common/parse` is cleanest, with codes mirrored between C and C++:\n- C: `enum { INT_PARSE_OK = 0, INT_PARSE_NOT_NUMBER = 1, INT_PARSE_OUT_OF_RANGE = 2 };` and `int parse_int32(const char* s, int32_t* out);`\n- C++: `inline constexpr int int_parse_not_number = 1; inline constexpr int int_parse_out_of_range = 2;` and `std::expected<std::int32_t,int> parse_int32(std::string_view s);`\n\nBut then callers (t1/t2 mains) must map codes to their own messages, exactly like t3-t4 maps parse_* codes. Good.\n\nHowever, there's a question: should the parser itself skip the leading '+'? Yes — shared logic. It must implement the safe rule: '+' allowed only if immediately followed by a digit.\n\nWait, but for the C side, strtol already handles '+'. The only reason to manual-skip is C++'s from_chars. If the common C implementation uses strtol directly, it doesn't need the skip. But then C and C++ grammars must match. The safe rule for C++ is: '+' must be followed by a digit. C's strtol naturally enforces that. So the two implementations converge. But to be safe and explicit, both could apply the same normalization.\n\nHmm, for C the out-of-range detection: errno==ERANGE || v<INT32_MIN || v>INT32_MAX.\n\nLet me now think whether a shared module is really better than two small functions in each language's string_utils. Given the user picked \"вынести общий парсер в common\", I'll go with common. I'll propose adding to existing string_utils to minimize CMake churn? But the user said \"общий парсер в common\" — could be string_utils. Let me think about what's most idiomatic and least surprising. `string_utils` currently = trim/is_empty/get_string. A number parser is a different concern. But creating a module for ~20 lines might be over-modularization which the AGENTS warns about. \n\nActually, the AGENTS § 4.2 says check STL before wrapper; and §4.1 п.9 says extract repeated logic. Extraction into a function is required; putting that function into a *new module* is a separate question. Simplest: add `parse_int32` to `string_utils` (C and C++), since it's already the shared text-processing module linked by t1/t2. No CMake target changes needed (t1/t2 already link string_utils in the relevant configs). This is the pragmatic choice. I'll recommend that in the plan, noting it can move to a dedicated `common/parse` later if it grows.\n\nWait — do t1/t2 link string_utils? Let me verify from CMake. Earlier journal: t1_cpp now links common_cpp_static WITH string_utils (2026-09-11 entry). Let me check root CMakeLists to confirm t1 and t2 link string_utils. I should verify before finalizing to avoid a wrong plan. Let me read root CMakeLists.txt.\n\nActually, I'm in plan mode; reading is allowed. Let me check.",
