          "text": "The user asks: strtol vs from_chars differ in 1. leading whitespace, 2. leading '+' sign, 3. overflow behavior. Anything else? They explicitly want only differences that affect THEIR TESTS (not internal API/read-range details).\n\nLet me review the probe outputs and the docs to enumerate behaviorally-observable differences relevant to t1-t2 integer parsing.\n\nFrom cppreference, from_chars integer differs from strtol (same base, default C locale):\n- \"0x\"/\"0X\" not recognized if base is 16 — NOT relevant (base 10, and strtol base 10 also doesn't recognize 0x). So no difference for our tests.\n- only minus sign recognized (not plus) — difference #2. Relevant (they patched with manual skip + guard).\n- leading whitespace not ignored — difference #1. Relevant, but both mains trim before parsing, so after trim it's moot; still it's a difference in raw functions.\n\nOverflow — #3. Actually, is overflow strictly a \"difference between the functions\"? Both signal out-of-range, but differently:\n- strtol: sets errno=ERANGE, returns clamped LONG_MAX/MIN, endptr still advances (consumes digits) → looks like a successful full conversion.\n- from_chars: ec=result_out_of_range, value untouched, ptr at first non-matching char (which for a digit string that overflows is... actually ptr points at the first char not matching the pattern; for pure-digit overflow, that's the end? Let's check the probe: line \"2147483648\": fc-raw ec=out_of_range full=0. full = (ec==ok && ptr==last). ptr is not printed. Hmm. Doesn't matter).\nSo difference #3 relevant.\n\nNow, \"anything else\" that affects tests. Let me think about observable differences for base-10 integer parsing over the test inputs:\n\n4. **hex** `0x10`: both strtol(base 10) and from_chars parse \"0\" then stop at 'x' → same. No difference. BUT note: if strtol used base 0 (auto), hex would differ; we use base 10. So not a difference for us. Worth stating to close it.\n\n5. **Value modification on error** — w